    "body": "<!-- claude-projects-attribution: {\"github_login\":\"siainbuletin\"} -->\n_Requested by **Sia** · [project thread](https://claude.ai/code/project/chan_01KaQfLZaF43QUMN3kcT7pW2?thread=cmsg_01KaQfLZaF43QUMN3kcT7pW2CeT7EXLucCBx8drQGW8YWR)_\n\nBefore: there was no place to track code issues, and working on a dotfiles branch meant checking it out in `~`, which swaps the live config.\n\nAfter: `M-x sp-org-issues-create-area` creates an \"Issues\" file as a vulpea-para area (tagged `area`, in the vulpea database), with one heading per repository (dotfiles, vulpea-mcp). `C-c c I` files an issue under a repository, `C-c o a i i` shows an issue board (in progress, next, waiting, open), and `M-x sp-org-issues-start` gives an issue its own git worktree under `~/src/dotfiles-wt/` and opens Magit there. For the dotfiles the worktree is added from the yadm repository itself, so `~` is never touched.\n\nHow: the file sets the org-gtd keywords (`TODO NEXT PROG WAIT | DONE CNCL`) with its own `#+todo:` line, and every issue carries `ORG_GTD: Actions`, so a NEXT issue also shows in `org-gtd-engage`. Repository settings (`GIT_DIR`, `WORKTREE_ROOT`, `BASE`) sit on the repository heading and are inherited by the issues. The module requires `sp-vulpea-para` first and appends its capture template and agenda commands, so that module's `setq` cannot wipe them. Closing an issue offers to remove its worktree, and Magit lists untracked files in yadm worktrees (the yadm repo hides them for `~`). `init.el` loads it right after `sp-org-ext`.\n\nThe literate source is `docs/tangles/sp-org-issues.org`; it tangles back to the `.el` byte for byte. Tested in batch Emacs with Org and current Magit against a repository laid out like yadm's (vulpea calls stubbed): area creation, capture into an existing and a missing repository heading, both agenda views, starting an issue from the agenda (branch, worktree, PROG), and removing the worktree on DONE.\n\n---\n_Generated by [Claude Code](https://claude.ai/code/session_0171DJqqHwkZapaFawFrrrAN)_",
